occupationalSummary:
&lt;div class=&quot;form-group&quot; style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0 0 12px; padding: 0; border: 0; font-variant-numeric: inherit; font-variant-east-asian: inherit; font-variant-alternates: inherit; font-variant-position: inherit; font-stretch: inherit; font-size: 16px; line-height: inherit&quot;&gt;
&lt;div style=&quot;box-sizing: inherit; margin: 0; padding: 0; border: 0; vertical-align: baseline&quot;&gt;Bus, coach and heavy goods vehicle (HGV) service and maintenance technicians work in the motor vehicle industry servicing and maintaining commercial goods and public service vehicles, such as HGVs, buses, and coaches. These vehicles play a critical role in the UK economy as the maintenance of supply chains is crucial to the transportation and distribution of goods such as fuel, food chain, construction, medication, and waste management. It can involve working in small or large independent garages, main dealers, manufacturers, or large fleet workshops. Service and maintenance technicians can either be based in a depot or they may work on a mobile basis, working out of a company van and travelling to customer sites to carry out service and repairs. They may also be expected to be on-call, where they will be required to attend urgent breakdowns to carry out repairs or inspections, sometimes out of normal working hours.&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;form-group&quot; style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0 0 12px; padding: 0; border: 0; font-variant-numeric: inherit; font-variant-east-asian: inherit; font-variant-alternates: inherit; font-variant-position: inherit; font-stretch: inherit; font-size: 16px; line-height: inherit&quot;&gt;The broad purpose of the occupation is to contribute to the efficient and effective running of commercial garages by performing routine vehicle maintenance inspections, pre-MOT inspections and vehicle checks on buses, coaches and HGVs, including diesel, hydrogen cell, battery and gas powered vehicles. This work is safety critical and must fully comply with legal requirements to make sure that operators maintain their required licenses to operate.&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;form-group&quot; style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0 0 12px; padding: 0; border: 0; font-variant-numeric: inherit; font-variant-east-asian: inherit; font-variant-alternates: inherit; font-variant-position: inherit; font-stretch: inherit; font-size: 16px; line-height: inherit&quot;&gt;Service and maintenance technicians interact with other members of the workshop team, including senior service and maintenance technicians, workshop managers, and customers.&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;form-group&quot; style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0 0 12px; padding: 0; border: 0; font-variant-numeric: inherit; font-variant-east-asian: inherit; font-variant-alternates: inherit; font-variant-position: inherit; font-stretch: inherit; font-size: 16px; line-height: inherit&quot;&gt;Technicians in this occupation are responsible for completing essential large commercial vehicle safety inspections, pre-MOT inspections, basic vehicle repairs and servicing to the legal standards required by road traffic, health and safety legislation and the legal undertakings to the Traffic Commissioner for operators&#39; licences. This role will include working with, and the safe storage and disposal of, hazardous materials and alternative propulsion sources, such as electric battery packs, liquified nitrogen gas or compressed natural gas (LNG or CNG) and hydrogen. A service and maintenance technician is responsible for the accurate completion of work packages in line with their job sheets and completion of supporting paperwork for regulatory bodies such as the Driver and Vehicle Standards Agency (DVSA).&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;
